# CardGate module for Magento 2

[![Total Downloads](https://img.shields.io/packagist/dt/cardgate/magento2.svg)](https://packagist.org/packages/cardgate/magento2)
[![Latest Version](https://img.shields.io/packagist/v/cardgate/magento2.svg)](https://github.com/cardgate/magento2/releases)
[![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/cardgate/magento2.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/cardgate/magento2)

## Support

This plugin supports Magento **2.1.x - 2.4.x**.

## Preparation

The usage of this module requires that you have obtained CardGate RESTful API credentials.
Please visit [My CardGate](https://my.cardgate.com/) and retrieve your credentials, or contact your accountmanager.

## Installation

The CardGate Module for Magento 2 is installed via [Composer](http://getcomposer.org/).

1. Go to Magento 2 root folder.

2. Enter following commands to install module:

   ```
   composer require cardgate/magento2
   ```

   Wait while dependencies are updated.

3. Enter following commands to enable module:

   ```
   php bin/magento module:enable Cardgate_Payment
   php bin/magento setup:upgrade
   php bin/magento setup:di:compile
   php bin/magento cache:clean
   ```

4. If Magento is running in production mode, deploy static content:

   ```
   php bin/magento setup:static-content:deploy
   ```

## Configuration

- Open the Magento Admin panel and view the CardGate plugin-configuration:
  - Stores > Configuration
  - Sales > CardGate
- Fill in the *CardGate configuration* section using the API credentials obtained earlier.
- Save config.
- Refresh the active payment methods:
  - *CardGate information* section
  - Payment methods > Refresh active payment methods
  - Check the output
- After the payment methods are refreshed successfully, all active *Payment method sections* can be enabled and edited.

## Subscriptions (CuroPayments recurring)

This module can sell products as recurring subscriptions. The first order is paid normally
by the customer; every following billing cycle is charged automatically through the
CuroPayments recurring entry point, without customer interaction:

- test: `https://secure-staging.curopayments.net/rest/v1/curo/recurring/`
- live: `https://secure.curopayments.net/rest/v1/curo/recurring/`

See [the CuroPayments documentation](https://curo.readme.io/reference/createrecurringtransaction)
for the entry point details. The initial (authorizing) transaction of the order acts as the
mandate for all following recurring charges.

### 1. Setup

1. Install/upgrade the module and run `php bin/magento setup:upgrade` (creates the
   `cardgate_subscription` table and the product attributes) followed by
   `php bin/magento cache:flush`.
2. Make sure the Magento cron is running (`bin/magento cron:run` from the system crontab).
   Subscription billing uses its **own cron group `cardgate`** (see `etc/cron_groups.xml`),
   so throttled settings of the `default` group do not delay billing.
3. Enable at least one of the allowed payment methods (see below).

### 2. Making a product a subscription

`setup:upgrade` adds three product attributes (group *CardGate Subscription*), available for
every product type (simple, configurable, bundle, grouped and virtual):

| Attribute | Code | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Sell as Subscription | `cardgate_is_subscription` | Yes = buying this product starts a subscription |
| Subscription Interval Count | `cardgate_subscription_interval` | Number of units between charges (minimum 1) |
| Subscription Interval Unit | `cardgate_subscription_interval_unit` | `day`, `week`, `month` or `year` (default `month`) |

Example: count `3` + unit `month` = billed every three months.

### 3. Cart and checkout rules

- **One subscription product per cart.** Adding a second subscription product is refused
  with the message *"Only one subscription product is allowed per cart. You can add
  non-subscription products."*
- **Mixed carts are allowed.** One-off products are billed only once by the initial order;
  only the subscription line-item is repeated.
- **Allowed payment methods.** A subscription is only created when the order is paid with:
  Bancontact (`cardgate_bancontact` / `cardgate_mistercash`), Creditcard, Direct Debit, iDEAL,
  Przelewy24 or onlineueberweisen. Orders paid with any other method are processed as normal
  orders without a subscription.

### 4. How a subscription is created

1. The customer places the order and pays the initial transaction at CardGate.
2. When the CardGate callback confirms the payment, the CardGate transaction id of the order
   is stored as the **mandate** and a subscription record is created for the subscription
   line-item (the `sales_order_place_after` observer tries too, but at that moment there is
   no mandate yet). Creation is idempotent: one subscription per order + product.
3. The subscription stores:
   - **amount**: row total incl. tax of the subscribed line-item;
   - **shipping amount**: the order shipping (incl. tax), scaled to the cycle length
     (cycle days / 30, limited between 0.25x and 2x);
   - currency, quantity, interval, payment method and mandate;
   - **next billing date** (`next_run_at`): order date + one interval, at 00:00 (UTC).

Renewal orders (created by a subscription) never create a new subscription.

### 5. Automated billing

The cron job `cardgate_process_subscriptions` runs **every 5 minutes** and picks up all
*active* subscriptions whose `next_run_at` has passed. For each of them:

1. If *skip next* is set, the flag is cleared and the cycle is moved forward one interval
   without charging.
2. Otherwise a **renewal order** is created: a copy of the subscribed line-item of the
   originating order, with the same customer, addresses, shipping and payment method.
3. The renewal order is charged through the recurring entry point using the mandate, the
   order grand total and the order increment id as reference.
4. The regular CardGate callback then captures and invoices the renewal order just like a
   normal order.
5. On success `cycles_billed` is increased, `last_order_id` / `last_transaction_id` /
   `last_run_at` are stored, failures are reset and `next_run_at` moves one interval forward.

### 6. Failures and retries

| Failure | What happens | Next attempt |
|---|---|---|
| Renewal order could not be created (gateway not contacted) | `failure_count` +1, `last_error` stored | 15, 30, 60, 120 ... minutes (max 240) |
| Gateway charge failed | Renewal order is cancelled with a comment, `failure_count` +1, `last_error` stored | 1, 2, 4, 7, 7 days (start of day) |

After **5 consecutive failures** the subscription is set to *paused* for review. Check
`last_error` in the admin and `var/log/exception.log` / `var/log/debug.log`, fix the cause and
resume the subscription.

### 7. Statuses

- **active** – billed automatically when due.
- **paused** – not billed. *Resume* resets the failures and schedules the next charge one
  interval from now.
- **cancelled** – final; never billed again and cannot be resumed or paused.

### 8. Customer self-service (*My Account → My Subscriptions*)

Logged-in customers can, for their own subscriptions:

- **Pause** / **Resume**;
- **Skip** the next billing cycle (active subscriptions only);
- **Change the interval** (shipping is rescaled and the next charge is scheduled one new
  interval from now);
- **Cancel**.

### 9. Administration (*Sales → Operations → CardGate Subscriptions*)

Requires the ACL resource *Sales → CardGate Subscriptions* (`Cardgate_Payment::subscriptions`);
grant it under *System → Permissions → User Roles* for non-administrator roles.

The grid lists all subscriptions. On the edit page an administrator can change:

- shipping amount per cycle;
- the *skip next* flag;
- the next billing date;
- the interval count/unit;
- the status (active / paused / cancelled).

### 10. Troubleshooting

- *Menu not visible*: run `setup:upgrade`, `cache:flush` and log in again.
- *Subscription not created*: check that the product has *Sell as Subscription* = Yes, the
  payment method is allowed and the CardGate callback reached the shop.
- *Nothing is billed*: check that the cron runs and the `cardgate` group has entries in
  `cron_schedule`, and that the subscription is *active* with a past `next_run_at`.

## Testing

The module ships with a PHPUnit test-suite that runs on PHP **7.4 up to 8.3**.

```
composer install
vendor/bin/phpunit
```

`phpunit.xml` uses the PHPUnit 10/11 schema and `tests/bootstrap.php` registers a
small PSR-4 autoloader so the standalone unit tests run without a full Magento
installation. PHPUnit `^10.5 || ^11.0` is declared under `require-dev`, both of
which support PHP 8.3.

## Requirements

No further requirements.
